�����"�
First of all, I want to say thank you to the author of this deck for compiling such a comprehensive list of vocabulary. It is evident that a ton of work was put into it. Kudos to you!

That being said, I'm not even 200 words and have found multiple errors that could hinder people's learning, amongst them:

- non-nouns being capitalized
- sentences that don't translate correctly
- stating that possessive pronouns ihr and Ihr are one and the same, and that capitalization doesn't matter (this being done implicitly, of course)

This is in no way, shape, or form a comprehensive list of the errors that I have found, since my focus is on learning the language, not finding ways to dunk on the incredible work done here, but finding these many mistakes before reaching the 1% mark of the total number of notes leads me to believe they will be many more to come and I should keep my eyes peeled.

A way for users to report — and maybe update themselves — instances where these mistakes happen may be beneficial for the community. A shared CSV file of the notes, for example, would be one of the many possible methods. This is of course not a demand, but a suggestion, and since nobody but me is responsible for my learning, I will updating my local version of the deck as needed.

Still choosing to leave a positive review because I believe there's a lot of value in this deck.*�	Thank you very much for taking the time to look through the deck so carefully and for leaving such a detailed comment. I really appreciate the effort and the positive tone of your feedback.You are right that a deck of 20,000 entries can contain mistakes, and I am already working on correcting the issues you mentioned. An updated version will be uploaded, but it will take some time because I want to revise everything systematically and ensure the changes are consistent.Regarding a few of the points you raised:

– Capitalization of non‑nouns: This is indeed an error and will be fixed.
– Sentence translations: Some sentences are not ideal and will be improved.
– “ihr” vs. “Ihr”: This seems to be a misunderstanding. In the deck, the possessive pronoun ihr and the formal pronoun Ihr are treated as separate entries. The capitalization is not meant to imply they are the same.

I am considering a way for users to report issues or contribute corrections (for example via a public CSV or GitHub), but I want to stabilize the base version first before opening it up for community edits.Thanks again for your constructive feedback and for leaving a positive review despite the issues. It genuinely helps improve the deck.0����
